  "question": {
    "text": "If a complex number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></math> lie on a circle of radius <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;units, then the complex number <math> <mrow> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mo>-</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>+</mo> <mn>4</mn> <mtext>z</mtext> </mrow> </math> will always lie on a circle of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math>&nbsp;units, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi></math> is equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let us assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></math> lies on a circle with centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> (fixed point) and radius <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> units.<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>&#8658; &#160;&#160;</mo> <mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>z</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br />Now, <math> <mrow> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mo>-</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>+</mo> <mn>4</mn> <mtext>z</mtext> <mo>&#8658;</mo> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>+</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>=</mo> <mn>4</mn> <mtext>z</mtext> </mrow> </math><br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mo>&#8658; &#160;&#160;</mo> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>+</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>-</mo> <mn>4</mn> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mn>4</mn> <mtext>z</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <mn>4</mn> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math><br /><br />Now, taking modulus on both sides, we get,<br /><br /><math> <mrow> <mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>+</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>-</mo> <mn>4</mn> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo>=</mo> <mn>4</mn> <mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mtext>z</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo>&#8658;</mo> <mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mi>&#969;</mi> <mo>+</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>-</mo> <mn>4</mn> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> <mo>=</mo> <mn>2</mn> </mrow> </math><br /><br />Locus of <math> <mrow> <mi>&#969;</mi> </mrow> </math> represents the circle having centre <math> <mrow> <mfenced close=\")\" open=\"(\" separators=\",\"> <mrow> <mo>-</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>+</mo> <mn>4</mn> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>0</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced> </mrow> </math> and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> units.",
